{
  "name": "What to Expect During the Rear Home Addition Timeline",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Planning and design coordination",
      "text": "During this initial window, you work with your contractor and design team to establish your budget, evaluate backyard setbacks, and draft architectural floor plans. The team checks existing site conditions, utilities, and structural attachment points to produce a buildable blueprint. This phase is handled collaboratively by you, the designer, and the contractor, typically taking about 1 to 3 months.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: City plan review and permit acquisition",
      "text": "Your contractor submits the finished architectural plans, engineering calculations, and Title 24 documentation to your local building department. Municipal plan checkers review the plans for structural integrity, lot-coverage limits, energy standards, and safety codes, issuing corrections if needed. The contractor handles these revisions with the city, a process that usually takes around 3 weeks to 3 months depending on local agency workloads.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Demolition, excavation, and foundation installation",
      "text": "Crews prepare the backyard work zone, demolish any old patios or decks, and excavate trenches for footings. Subcontractors run underground plumbing lines before forms are built, rebar is placed, and concrete is poured and cured. This work is managed on-site by your builder and trade specialists, typically taking about 2 to 4 weeks.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Framing, roofing, and weather enclosure",
      "text": "Framers construct the subfloor, erect exterior and interior walls, set roof trusses, and tie the new structure into your home’s existing roof and siding. Roofers install underlayment and shingles, while carpenters install exterior doors and windows to make the structure weather-tight. The framing and exterior envelope phase is led by the general contractor's carpentry crew and usually takes 3 to 6 weeks.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Rough mechanicals and city rough inspections",
      "text": "Electricians, plumbers, and HVAC technicians run wiring, pipes, and ductwork through the open wall cavities. The contractor schedules city inspectors to inspect and approve all framing and rough mechanical systems before any insulation goes in. This trade rough-in and inspection phase is directed by the general contractor and generally spans 2 to 4 weeks.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 6: Insulation, drywall, and interior finishes",
      "text": "Insulation is installed and inspected, followed by drywall hanging, taping, texturing, and painting. Finish carpenters then lay flooring, install trim, hang interior doors, set cabinetry, and mount lighting fixtures and plumbing trim. The contractor coordinates finish trades to ensure tight detailing, taking roughly 4 to 8 weeks.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 7: Final inspections and project completion",
      "text": "The general contractor calls in final city inspections for building, electrical, plumbing, and mechanical permits to secure your final sign-off. You walk through the completed space with your builder, resolve any punch-list items, and receive care instructions for new finishes. This wrap-up phase takes about 1 to 2 weeks. Want to map out your own rear addition project? Contact Hometelligent Inc. at 925-383-8163 to talk through your timeline.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 7
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
  "totalTime": "PT30M",
